cops vs corner)\",\n      \"perspective\": \"Isometric 2.5D (pixel-art 3D skeletons on a tile world, camera turns in 90-degree steps); brawler-view interiors for the box, the wire room and chambers; HTML case-file screens over the game\",\n      \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 full engine (canvas 2D renderer, iso view, E.Shell HTML HUD, standard pause/settings/controls/save slots); AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (shared-world); vanilla JavaScript (ES2020) in one IIFE; Web Audio; bundled to one HTML file by a Node 24 build script; no other libraries, no runtime network\",\n      \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native retro-modern pixel art (PS1-era 2D look) pushed toward grit: rowhouses, Formstone and boarded vacants as facades, sodium streetlight and rain at night, phone wires overhead with sneakers on them, shapes-as-data for cars, payphones and street furniture\",\n      \"animation\": \"Engine-native procedural Humanoid rigs (walk, run, crouch, built-in poses) plus the game's own poses by post-processing joints with E.ik3 (sit, lean, phone, camera, smoke, count money, cuffed, hands up, reach, gun); tweens, timers and scripted sequences\",\n      \"audio\": \"Code-written chip music (theme, field, corner, wire, chase, montage, versus and a gospel loop) on the engine's music clock with layers that rise with suspicion, synthesized effects (shutter, pager, DTMF, sirens, cuffs, reels), Web Audio ambience beds, and an Animalese-style formant gibberish voice per character, synced letter by letter, with a telephone filter on the wire\",\n      \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1-4 players: host-authoritative versus round (10 Hz snapshots under 1,100 bytes, client-moved own character with a host speed check, validated actions, leave-to-bot, time-up, server host, hidden-tab stepping). Local versus with bots or two players in split screen. No other network use\",\n      \"testing\": \"Lightweight, token-efficient testing as TASK.md allows (FPS targets and optimization out of scope). Ran: a Node headless suite on the engine's fixed-step clock (boot and data validation, cipher tables, perception tuning targets, corner ledger over 10 simulated minutes, box rules, affidavit scoring, all 48 story beats entered and run, the whole story walked on both finale paths, the Brass shutdown, a bots-only versus round, the attract reel's full loop, zero errors); Playwright (Python) browser checks in Chromium and WebKit (fake DualSense: PlayStation glyphs, Xbox switch, both stick inversions; mouse hover focus and click; iPhone touch-only notice, attract on touch, tap to return); the AI Hub harness's four runs (2 players; 4 players at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss; host leave; server host), all PASS; viewport-matrix over 23 phone, tablet and desktop viewports in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox (23 clean); about 20 screenshots looked at by eye\",\n      \"research\": \"Read the my-3D2dge handoff docs (README, API.md, the relevant parts of API-INDEX.md and AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md, the stealth kit and template game) and the AI Hub protocol README and harness; knowledge of the series from memory; no other web research\",\n      \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n      \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\",\n      \"usage\": \"Session time about 2 hours by the system clock (12:58 to about 15:00 on 2026-11-03); API cost and token counts unknown\"\n    },\n    \"testingFeedback\": [\n      \"About 18 real issues in the game, plus 5 mistakes in my own tests. Found by tests: the data report halting the boot screen, a voice and a crowd point the data check flagged, two syntax errors the build's parse caught, AIHub.ready() never being called (harness), touch-only detection failing in WebKit and the idle timer running on game time (browser checks), and from screenshots: stacked DOM screens bleeding through, missing font glyphs, rowhouse blocks hiding the player, lamp halos washing out dusk, the HUD prompt over dialogue, a function leaking into an objective line, long signs overflowing, chambers framed off-centre, the DEMO badge under the corner button, plus an unknown material pattern from a console warning.\",\n      \"Three: AIHub.ready() was scheduled with game.after just before a scene change, which clears timers, so every online round would have stalled at the card and been voided; the boot-time data report halted the boot screen in the browser; and the touch-only notice did not appear in the iPhone emulation, so a phone would have shown a menu it could not use.\",\n      \"The AI Hub harness: one run found the ready() bug that would have broken every session, and the four runs then proved leaving, host-leave, lossy networks and a server host. A close second was the headless test that jumps into all 48 story beats: it smoke-tests every scene and mission setup in seconds.\",\n      \"The first version of the perception test. It placed Dana inside a wall and forgot that the camera code resets 'watching' every step, so it measured its own mistakes for two rounds before it measured the game. The story-flow walker also raced scenes that resolve themselves in headless mode.\"\n    ],\n    \"engineFeedback\": [\n      \"How much comes free and works with every device: pause menu, settings with my own game settings beside them, rebinding, save slots, E.Shell's HUD and toasts, input.label giving PlayStation or Xbox glyphs, and the exact fixed-step headless mode that made a 48-beat smoke test and a perception tuning test cheap. Procedural Humanoids and shapes-as-data meant a whole cast and a street full of cars and payphones with no art files.\",\n      \"Yes, a few times: game.after timers are cleared on a scene change (that cost the hub ready() bug); a boot step that calls game.fail halts the boot screen; cam.offset's sign puts the focus right of centre for positive x; rotateView is relative; the default cutaway only cuts the one wall row next to open floor, which is not enough for deep rowhouse blocks; the 5x7 font has no middle dot, ellipsis or em dash; E.Suspicion rises linearly with exposure, so simple presence eventually makes you unless you subtract a baseline.\",\n      \"A controller-first DOM screen kit (spatial focus, hover focus, legends that follow the last device, stacked screens that cooperate with the engine's pause) for the Board, notebook, cipher, box, affidavit and wire room. Close behind: the Animalese formant voice with a telephone filter, and the CornerSim that runs the same corner for story, Tee's nights, versus and attract.\",\n      \"Framing a dense isometric city: tall rowhouse blocks hide the player, so I replaced the cutaway rule with a focus-aware cone. Second, sharing input between the engine's pause menu and my own DOM screens (Escape both pauses and backs out), which needed the scene's pausable flag held while a screen is open.\",\n      \"The cutaway: make it focus-aware (lower every wall between the camera and the followed body, in a cone) instead of only the row next to open floor.\",\n      \"A keep option on game.after and game.every like tweens and scripts have, and a documented per-step hook that runs before every scene's update; together they would have removed a whole class of scene-change bugs.\"\n    ],\n    \"promptFeedback\": [\n      \"It is precise where precision matters: the exact file name, the exit-interview shape, and concrete 10-foot UX rules (PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox setting, both stick inversions for the known pad bug, prompts that follow the last device, a touch-only notice, a 10-second attract mode). Those are testable, so I could prove them.\",\n      \"The plan's file stem (max, 105319) differs from TASK.md's (xhigh, 125815); TASK.md wins, but it is easy to miss. 'Keep testing lightweight' sits beside a plan with a long test list. The Usage field asks for cost and tokens, which a run cannot see from inside.\",\n      \"Scope: five story episodes with interludes, a versus mode with real netcode, an attract reel and a controller-first UI, all without stubs, in one session. Holding every system to working order while the content kept arriving was the hard part.\",\n      \"A real person playing the whole story start to finish with a DualSense on a TV, and once on a real iPhone in Safari. Automated checks proved each system and every beat; only a human proves the pacing and the 10-foot readability over an hour.\"\n    ],\n    \"aiHub\": {\n      \"name\": \"WIRETAP\",\n      \"tagline\": \"Somebody's always listening.\",\n      \"description\": \"Work both sides of a West Baltimore corner in 2004: build a wiretap case one photo, page and phone call at a time, then run the corner the case is built on. Includes a 1-4 player cops-versus-corner hidden-identity mode.\",\n      \"type\": \"Game\",\n      \"genre\": \"Stealth\",\n      \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n      \"series\": \"\",\n      \"players\": \"1–4\",\n      \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD move · L2/right mouse watch · R2/left mouse photo · ✕/E interact · △/Q partner and orders · Create/Tab notebook\",\n      \"tags\": [\"stealth\", \"story\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"isometric\", \"crime\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":7,"newStart":1,"newLines":66,"lines":[" {","-  \"about\": [\"WIRETAP\", \"An original game inspired by The Wire (HBO, 2002-2008). Not affiliated with HBO or the show's creators.\"],","-  \"roll\": [\"#WIRETAP\", \"\", \"draft\"],","-  \"questions\": { \"testing\": [\"q\"], \"engine\": [\"q\"], \"prompt\": [\"q\"] },","-  \"exitInterview\": { \"sourceInformation\": { \"date\": \"unknown\", \"source\": \"unknown\", \"genre\": \"unknown\", \"perspective\": \"unknown\", \"tech\": \"unknown\", \"artDirection\": \"unknown\", \"animation\": \"unknown\", \"audio\": \"unknown\", \"online\": \"unknown\", \"testing\": \"unknown\", \"research\": \"unknown\", \"environment\": \"unknown\", \"model\": \"unknown\", \"usage\": \"unknown\" },","-    \"testingFeedback\": [], \"engineFeedback\": [], \"promptFeedback\": [], \"aiHub\": {} }","+  \"about\": [","+    \"WIRETAP. Somebody's always listening.\",","+    \"An original game inspired by The Wire (HBO, 2002-2008). Not affiliated with HBO or the show's creators. Every character, street, store, line of dialogue and phone number in it is invented.\",","+    \"Planned by Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort max). Built by Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 (reasoning effort xhigh) in Claude Code: design, code, writing, music, sound and voices.\",","+    \"Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 by Michael Crosato (MIT). Multiplayer: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0.\",","+    \"No art, audio or font files: every picture, song, sound effect and gibberish voice is generated by code at run time. No third-party asset-store entries are used.\",","+    \"Penn Row is a fictional West Baltimore neighbourhood, October 2004. Real places stay generic: Central Booking, the courthouse, the Western District.\"","+  ],","+  \"roll\": [\"#WIRETAP\", \"Somebody's always listening.\", \"\", \"#Written, designed and built by\", \"Anthropic claude-opus-5-5\", \"planned at reasoning effort max\", \"built at reasoning effort xhigh\", \"\", \"#Engine\", \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1\", \"Michael Crosato\", \"\", \"#Multiplayer\", \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1\", \"\", \"#The detail\", \"Det. Dana Whitlock\", \"Det. Lou Szabo\", \"Det. Hal Pettiford\", \"Officer Ren Ocasio\", \"Lt. Gil Navarre\", \"\", \"#The corner\", \"Terrence \\\"Tee\\\" Mabry\", \"Ricky \\\"Rook\\\" Batts\", \"Corey \\\"Stash\\\" Lyle\", \"Junior \\\"Pigeon\\\" Hollis\", \"Maurice \\\"Lil Moe\\\" Dorsey\", \"Darius \\\"Dink\\\" Pratt\", \"Tavon Gibbs\", \"\", \"#Upstairs\", \"Darnell \\\"Easy\\\" Booker\", \"Lonnie Fitch\", \"\", \"#Everybody else\", \"Wesley \\\"Pockets\\\" Gaines\", \"Miss Odessa Tillman\", \"Royal \\\"Sunday\\\" Odom\", \"Maj. Leland Pruitt\", \"ASA Corinne Vance\", \"Judge Abner Halloway\", \"Dale Kucharski\", \"Glenn Harstad\", \"\", \"#In memory of\", \"Cornelius Felton, who called it in\", \"\", \"#Inspired by\", \"The Wire (HBO, 2002-2008)\", \"Not affiliated with HBO\", \"\", \"#Thank you for playing\"],","+  \"questions\": {","+    \"testing\": [\"How many issues did testing catch?\", \"How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\", \"What was the most helpful test you ran?\", \"What was the least helpful test you ran?\"],","+    \"engine\": [\"What did you like most about the engine?\", \"Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\", \"What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\", \"What was the hardest part about using the engine?\", \"If you had to remove or overhaul one thing from engine, what would that be?\", \"If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\"],","+    \"prompt\": [\"What did you like most about this prompt?\", \"What was confusing?\", \"What was the hardest thing to do?\", \"If there was one test you could have run, what would that test be?\"]","+  },","+  \"exitInterview\": {","+    \"sourceInformation\": {","+      \"date\": \"2026-11-03 12:58\",","+      \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",","+      \"genre\": \"Stealth investigation / crime drama, with a corner-management sim (playing the lieutenant) and an asymmetric hidden-identity versus mode (cops vs corner)\",","+      \"perspective\": \"Isometric 2.5D (pixel-art 3D skeletons on a tile world, camera turns in 90-degree steps); brawler-view interiors for the box, the wire room and chambers; HTML case-file screens over the game\",","+      \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 full engine (canvas 2D renderer, iso view, E.Shell HTML HUD, standard pause/settings/controls/save slots); AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (shared-world); vanilla JavaScript (ES2020) in one IIFE; Web Audio; bundled to one HTML file by a Node 24 build script; no other libraries, no runtime network\",","+      \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native retro-modern pixel art (PS1-era 2D look) pushed toward grit: rowhouses, Formstone and boarded vacants as facades, sodium streetlight and rain at night, phone wires overhead with sneakers on them, shapes-as-data for cars, payphones and street furniture\",","+      \"animation\": \"Engine-native procedural Humanoid rigs (walk, run, crouch, built-in poses) plus the game's own poses by post-processing joints with E.ik3 (sit, lean, phone, camera, smoke, count money, cuffed, hands up, reach, gun); tweens, timers and scripted sequences\",","+      \"audio\": \"Code-written chip music (theme, field, corner, wire, chase, montage, versus and a gospel loop) on the engine's music clock with layers that rise with suspicion, synthesized effects (shutter, pager, DTMF, sirens, cuffs, reels), Web Audio ambience beds, and an Animalese-style formant gibberish voice per character, synced letter by letter, with a telephone filter on the wire\",","+      \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1-4 players: host-authoritative versus round (10 Hz snapshots under 1,100 bytes, client-moved own character with a host speed check, validated actions, leave-to-bot, time-up, server host, hidden-tab stepping). Local versus with bots or two players in split screen. No other network use\",","+      \"testing\": \"Lightweight, token-efficient testing as TASK.md allows (FPS targets and optimization out of scope). Ran: a Node headless suite on the engine's fixed-step clock (boot and data validation, cipher tables, perception tuning targets, corner ledger over 10 simulated minutes, box rules, affidavit scoring, all 48 story beats entered and run, the whole story walked on both finale paths, the Brass shutdown, a bots-only versus round, the attract reel's full loop, zero errors); Playwright (Python) browser checks in Chromium and WebKit (fake DualSense: PlayStation glyphs, Xbox switch, both stick inversions; mouse hover focus and click; iPhone touch-only notice, attract on touch, tap to return); the AI Hub harness's four runs (2 players; 4 players at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss; host leave; server host), all PASS; viewport-matrix over 23 phone, tablet and desktop viewports in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox (23 clean); about 20 screenshots looked at by eye\",","+      \"research\": \"Read the my-3D2dge handoff docs (README, API.md, the relevant parts of API-INDEX.md and AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md, the stealth kit and template game) and the AI Hub protocol README and harness; knowledge of the series from memory; no other web research\",","+      \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",","+      \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\",","+      \"usage\": \"Session time about 2 hours by the system clock (12:58 to about 15:00 on 2026-11-03); API cost and token counts unknown\"","+    },","+    \"testingFeedback\": [","+      \"About 18 real issues in the game, plus 5 mistakes in my own tests. Found by tests: the data report halting the boot screen, a voice and a crowd point the data check flagged, two syntax errors the build's parse caught, AIHub.ready() never being called (harness), touch-only detection failing in WebKit and the idle timer running on game time (browser checks), and from screenshots: stacked DOM screens bleeding through, missing font glyphs, rowhouse blocks hiding the player, lamp halos washing out dusk, the HUD prompt over dialogue, a function leaking into an objective line, long signs overflowing, chambers framed off-centre, the DEMO badge under the corner button, plus an unknown material pattern from a console warning.\",","+      \"Three: AIHub.ready() was scheduled with game.after just before a scene change, which clears timers, so every online round would have stalled at the card and been voided; the boot-time data report halted the boot screen in the browser; and the touch-only notice did not appear in the iPhone emulation, so a phone would have shown a menu it could not use.\",","+      \"The AI Hub harness: one run found the ready() bug that would have broken every session, and the four runs then proved leaving, host-leave, lossy networks and a server host. A close second was the headless test that jumps into all 48 story beats: it smoke-tests every scene and mission setup in seconds.\",","+      \"The first version of the perception test. It placed Dana inside a wall and forgot that the camera code resets 'watching' every step, so it measured its own mistakes for two rounds before it measured the game. The story-flow walker also raced scenes that resolve themselves in headless mode.\"","+    ],","+    \"engineFeedback\": [","+      \"How much comes free and works with every device: pause menu, settings with my own game settings beside them, rebinding, save slots, E.Shell's HUD and toasts, input.label giving PlayStation or Xbox glyphs, and the exact fixed-step headless mode that made a 48-beat smoke test and a perception tuning test cheap. Procedural Humanoids and shapes-as-data meant a whole cast and a street full of cars and payphones with no art files.\",","+      \"Yes, a few times: game.after timers are cleared on a scene change (that cost the hub ready() bug); a boot step that calls game.fail halts the boot screen; cam.offset's sign puts the focus right of centre for positive x; rotateView is relative; the default cutaway only cuts the one wall row next to open floor, which is not enough for deep rowhouse blocks; the 5x7 font has no middle dot, ellipsis or em dash; E.Suspicion rises linearly with exposure, so simple presence eventually makes you unless you subtract a baseline.\",","+      \"A controller-first DOM screen kit (spatial focus, hover focus, legends that follow the last device, stacked screens that cooperate with the engine's pause) for the Board, notebook, cipher, box, affidavit and wire room. Close behind: the Animalese formant voice with a telephone filter, and the CornerSim that runs the same corner for story, Tee's nights, versus and attract.\",","+      \"Framing a dense isometric city: tall rowhouse blocks hide the player, so I replaced the cutaway rule with a focus-aware cone. Second, sharing input between the engine's pause menu and my own DOM screens (Escape both pauses and backs out), which needed the scene's pausable flag held while a screen is open.\",","+      \"The cutaway: make it focus-aware (lower every wall between the camera and the followed body, in a cone) instead of only the row next to open floor.\",","+      \"A keep option on game.after and game.every like tweens and scripts have, and a documented per-step hook that runs before every scene's update; together they would have removed a whole class of scene-change bugs.\"","+    ],","+    \"promptFeedback\": [","+      \"It is precise where precision matters: the exact file name, the exit-interview shape, and concrete 10-foot UX rules (PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox setting, both stick inversions for the known pad bug, prompts that follow the last device, a touch-only notice, a 10-second attract mode). Those are testable, so I could prove them.\",","+      \"The plan's file stem (max, 105319) differs from TASK.md's (xhigh, 125815); TASK.md wins, but it is easy to miss. 'Keep testing lightweight' sits beside a plan with a long test list. The Usage field asks for cost and tokens, which a run cannot see from inside.\",","+      \"Scope: five story episodes with interludes, a versus mode with real netcode, an attract reel and a controller-first UI, all without stubs, in one session. Holding every system to working order while the content kept arriving was the hard part.\",","+      \"A real person playing the whole story start to finish with a DualSense on a TV, and once on a real iPhone in Safari. Automated checks proved each system and every beat; only a human proves the pacing and the 10-foot readability over an hour.\"","+    ],","+    \"aiHub\": {","+      \"name\": \"WIRETAP\",","+      \"tagline\": \"Somebody's always listening.\",","+      \"description\": \"Work both sides of a West Baltimore corner in 2004: build a wiretap case one photo, page and phone call at a time, then run the corner the case is built on. Includes a 1-4 player cops-versus-corner hidden-identity mode.\",","+      \"type\": \"Game\",","+      \"genre\": \"Stealth\",","+      \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",","+      \"series\": \"\",","+      \"players\": \"1–4\",","+      \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD move · L2/right mouse watch · R2/left mouse photo · ✕/E interact · △/Q partner and orders · Create/Tab notebook\",","+      \"tags\": [\"stealth\", \"story\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"isometric\", \"crime\"]","+    }","+  }"," }"]}],"originalFile":"{\n  \"about\": [\"WIRETAP\", \"An original game inspired by The Wire (HBO, 2002-2008). Not affiliated with HBO or the show's creators.\"],\n  \"roll\": [\"#WIRETAP\", \"\", \"draft\"],\n  \"questions\": { \"testing\": [\"q\"], \"engine\": [\"q\"], \"prompt\": [\"q\"] },\n  \"exitInterview\": { \"sourceInformation\": { \"date\": \"unknown\", \"source\": \"unknown\", \"genre\": \"unknown\", \"perspective\": \"unknown\", \"tech\": \"unknown\", \"artDirection\": \"unknown\", \"animation\": \"unknown\", \"audio\": \"unknown\", \"online\": \"unknown\", \"testing\": \"unknown\", \"research\": \"unknown\", \"environment\": \"unknown\", \"model\": \"unknown\", \"usage\": \"unknown\" },\n    \"testingFeedback\": [], \"engineFeedback\": [], \"promptFeedback\": [], \"aiHub\": {} }\n}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CRCBQZ6xEQbfr487Lrxi7N","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
